{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nrebar exposes shared functionality through CLI, MCP, and the Python library. The bridge redesign is\nnot complete until the noun-based operations are available on all three surfaces. This ticket adds\nbridge-shaped MCP tools and library functions after the CLI/core prerequisites land.\n\nThis is additive. Existing public callers depend on `rebar.reconcile(mode=\"dry-run\")`, the MCP\n`reconcile(mode=...)` tool, and `rebar.bridge_fsck()`. Those interfaces remain functional\ncompatibility entrypoints with their published defaults and schemas. New bridge operations become\nthe primary documented API, while old clients continue to work. Any future removal requires a\nseparate deprecation decision and evidence that consumers have migrated.\n\nThe defaults cannot be changed by a silent alias:\n\n- `rebar.reconcile()` defaults to dry-run.\n- the MCP `reconcile` tool receives an explicit mode and gates mutating modes through\n  `mcp.allow_jira_sync`.\n- `bridge_sync()` is explicitly mutating.\n- `bridge_preview()` is explicitly non-mutating.\n\nCompatibility adapters therefore preserve legacy signatures/defaults and normalize into the same\ncore request used by the new operations.\n\nSibling `9895-aa2c-8907-4db6` owns the typed core disposition, canonical 0/1/2 mapping, and\nlegacy exit-compatibility translations. This ticket consumes that policy; it does not reimplement\nor flatten compatibility behavior.\n`f789` remains dependency-ordered after `9895`.\n\n## Additive library surface\n\nExpose typed public functions for machine-usable bridge operations, reusing the shared core rather\nthan launching a second implementation:\n\n- `bridge_preview(...)`: non-mutating preview.\n- `bridge_sync(...)`: mutating sync, with explicit limits/options rather than a mode string.\n- `bridge_status(...)`: durable status and optional max-age assertion from `b69c`.\n- `bridge_pause(reason, ...)` / `bridge_resume(...)`: durable operator controls from `1104`.\n- `bridge_fsck(...)`: retain the already-published function and align its documentation with the\n  new CLI vocabulary.\n- `bridge_check_access(...)`: machine-usable access check where the underlying operation has a\n  structured result.\n\nThe interactive setup wizard remains a CLI interaction unless it already has a noninteractive\ntyped result contract; do not invent an MCP/library prompt protocol.\n\n`rebar.reconcile(mode=...)` remains exported with its existing default and accepted mode values.\nIt maps to the same core preview/sync request while preserving observable output, mutation\nboundaries, exceptions, and return type. `rebar.bridge_fsck()` remains callable unchanged.\n\n## Additive MCP surface\n\nPublish MCP tools paralleling the machine-usable bridge operations. New tools have explicit typed\ninputs and no mode parameter. Existing `reconcile(mode=...)` and `bridge_fsck` tools remain\nregistered with their existing names and input/output contracts.\n\n`mcp.allow_jira_sync` gates every mutating route, including `bridge_sync`, `bridge_pause`,\n`bridge_resume`, and mutating legacy `reconcile` modes. It must not gate preview, status, fsck,\nor access-check reads beyond their existing authorization requirements.\n\nEvery structured-result tool publishes an `outputSchema`, updates the MCP model/type registries,\nand passes generated reference/schema parity checks. Compatibility tools retain their published\nschemas so older MCP clients do not fail schema negotiation.\n\n## Shared implementation and error policy\n\nLibrary and MCP adapters call one reusable core operation layer. They do not shell out through the\nCLI and do not copy the exit-code table. The typed disposition from `9895` is translated once into canonical 0/1/2 return/error behavior\nfor new CLI, library, and MCP operations. Compatibility adapters consume the same disposition but\npreserve their published external return/exception contracts.\n\nPause/resume use the same CAS and identity contracts proven by `1104`; status consumes the durable\nstate contract from `b69c`; fsck consumes `030f`; preview/sync consume the final core from\n`424f` and `4a44`.\n\n## Test plan\n\nExercise each new operation through the public import path and through the real MCP registration\nand schema boundary. For every compatibility mapping, run representative old and new calls against the same temporary\nrepository and assert equal semantic state and mutation boundaries while separately asserting each\nlegacy adapter's published output/return/exception contract and intentionally distinct defaults.\n\nCompatibility matrix:\n\n- `rebar.reconcile()` -> non-mutating behavior equivalent to `bridge_preview()`, while keeping\n  the old signature/default.\n- each explicit legacy mode -> its mapped preview/sync operation and limit from `4a44`.\n- MCP `reconcile(mode=...)` -> the same mapped new tool behavior.\n- existing library/MCP `bridge_fsck` -> unchanged result and schema.\n- old published input/output schemas -> still accepted after new tools register.\n\nTest authorization both ways: all mutating new and legacy MCP paths are rejected when\n`mcp.allow_jira_sync` is false, and all intended read-only paths remain callable. Test the shared semantic disposition through CLI, library, and MCP, proving canonical operations\nuse the new mapping and compatibility operations retain their old external contract, without\nsource/layout assertions.\n\nSurface parity census:\n\n- CLI: continuity/reference control; behavior is implemented by prerequisite stories.\n- Library: in scope through public imports and typed return/error contracts.\n- MCP: in scope through actual tool registration, input models, output schemas, and auth gates.\n- Automated callers: in scope for existing library/MCP consumers through compatibility tests and\n  generated references.\n- Dual direction: in scope for preview/sync, pause/resume, and status/fsck read/write pairs.\n\nNo new contention harness is required: adapters use the already-tested core CAS/lock seams and add\nno retry, cache, or composed store write.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every machine-usable bridge operation has a public library function and MCP tool backed by\n      the same reusable core behavior as the CLI.\n- [ ] New bridge MCP input schemas use explicit options and contain no mode vocabulary.\n- [ ] `rebar.reconcile(mode=...)` remains exported, retains its dry-run default, accepted values,\n      return type, and exception behavior, and has the same semantic state/mutation outcome as the\n      mapped new bridge operations.\n- [ ] The MCP `reconcile(mode=...)` tool remains registered with its existing schema/output/error\n      contract and has the same semantic state/mutation outcome as the mapped new bridge tools.\n- [ ] Existing library and MCP `bridge_fsck` entrypoints remain callable with unchanged result and\n      schema contracts while current docs use the noun-based CLI spelling.\n- [ ] Compatibility adapters preserve old return types, exceptions, output schemas, mutation\n      boundaries, and defaults; they do not silently turn preview into sync.\n- [ ] `_lib_ops.py` consumes the shared core disposition from `9895`; canonical CLI/library/MCP\n      mappings match without copied whitelists, while compatibility adapters retain their published\n      external translations.\n- [ ] Every new structured-result MCP tool publishes an output schema and passes model/type/schema\n      generation and coverage checks; existing compatibility schemas remain valid.\n- [ ] `mcp.allow_jira_sync` gates all and only mutating new and compatibility routes.\n- [ ] Public API and MCP documentation presents the new bridge operations as primary and documents\n      retained compatibility mappings without instructing consumers to break immediately.\n- [ ] No existing library or MCP environment breaks merely because it still calls\n      `reconcile(mode=...)` or `bridge_fsck`.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py`: new bridge library operations plus legacy reconcile compatibility over\n  the shared core.\n- `src/rebar/__init__.py`: export the additive public functions while retaining old exports.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`: new bridge MCP tools and retained compatibility registrations.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py`: typed inputs/outputs for new tools without changing old schemas.\n- `src/rebar/mcp_server.py`: register additive tools and preserve old names.\n- `src/rebar/types.py`: public structured result types where required.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/`: output schemas and generated parity for new machine results.\n- `tests/interfaces/facades/`, `tests/interfaces/contracts/`, and focused unit tests: public\n  import, MCP registration/auth/schema, cross-surface disposition, and compatibility parity.\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md`, `docs/reuse-surface.md`, `docs/user-guide.md`, and\n  `docs/release-notes.md`: primary APIs plus compatibility contract.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786215165270694001,"uuid":"6b8b911a-9071-4ed6-a353-58be27610956"}